<section class="dr-mast"><div class="dr-w"><p class="dr-iss">Issue No. 1 &middot; The Fabric That Moves</p><p class="dr-big" aria-hidden="true">DRAPING</p><div class="dr-mast-g"><h1>The quiet art of letting fabric fall.</h1><p class="dr-lead">Draping is an independent fashion journal about wraps, scarves, sarees, cowls, pleats and folds: how fabric hangs, how to style it on the body, and how to choose cloth that moves the way you want.</p></div></div>
<div class="dr-tri"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793668211105-8e6aaa46e36c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=620&q=75" alt="woman in a long black dress on a sandy beach" width="420" height="620" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>I. The long line</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793867146947-843fd080cd30?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=620&q=75" alt="woman in a dark blue sequined saree" width="420" height="620" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>II. The wrapped body</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794562386150-e08127247777?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=620&q=75" alt="woman in a shimmering olive green gown" width="420" height="620" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>III. The gown that flows</figcaption></figure></div></section>

<section class="dr-s"><div class="dr-w"><div class="dr-sh"><span>Chapter one</span><h2>The lookbook: five drapes to try</h2><p>Scroll sideways. Each look takes three moves.</p></div></div><div class="dr-strip" tabindex="0" aria-label="Lookbook"><div class="dr-look"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794905797536-07177d90b94c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=460&q=75" alt="person in a white pleated outfit with red accessories" width="360" height="460" loading="lazy"></figure><div><span>Look 01</span><h3>The one-shoulder wrap</h3><ol><li>Hold one corner at the opposite hip</li><li>Take the length over one shoulder</li><li>Tuck the end at the back of the waist</li></ol></div></div><div class="dr-look"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794130681931-21132fa3e439?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=460&q=75" alt="young woman in a floral dress and straw hat" width="360" height="460" loading="lazy"></figure><div><span>Look 02</span><h3>The loose neck loop</h3><ol><li>Fold a long scarf in half lengthwise</li><li>Loop it once around the neck</li><li>Leave both ends long and uneven</li></ol></div></div><div class="dr-look"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793980948495-5f7ea4e67576?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=460&q=75" alt="woman in a light blue matching set" width="360" height="460" loading="lazy"></figure><div><span>Look 03</span><h3>The belted layer</h3><ol><li>Drape a long shawl over both shoulders</li><li>Let the fronts fall straight</li><li>Belt it at the waist to hold the shape</li></ol></div></div><div class="dr-look"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794905951798-2a40e75a56a8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=460&q=75" alt="woman in a white pleated top with red earrings" width="360" height="460" loading="lazy"></figure><div><span>Look 04</span><h3>The cowl front</h3><ol><li>Choose a soft, bias-cut fabric</li><li>Let the neckline fold into soft curves</li><li>Keep jewellery small so the folds lead</li></ol></div></div><div class="dr-look"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794562386159-c579f0139474?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=460&q=75" alt="woman in a sequined olive dress" width="360" height="460" loading="lazy"></figure><div><span>Look 05</span><h3>The evening fall</h3><ol><li>Pick a fluid, heavy fabric</li><li>Let it skim, not cling</li><li>Add a single shoulder detail</li></ol></div></div></div></section>

<section class="dr-s dr-cream" id="drape-o-meter"><div class="dr-w dr-meter"><div><div class="dr-sh"><span>Chapter two</span><h2>The drape-o-meter</h2><p>Choose a fabric and watch how it hangs.</p></div><div class="dr-chips" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Fabric">
<label><input type="radio" name="fab" value="0" checked><span>Silk charmeuse</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="fab" value="1"><span>Viscose crepe</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="fab" value="2"><span>Cotton jersey</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="fab" value="3"><span>Linen</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="fab" value="4"><span>Cotton poplin</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="fab" value="5"><span>Wool felt</span></label></div><div class="dr-res" id="dm-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<svg class="dr-curt" viewBox="0 0 300 320" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="280" y2="20" stroke="#231C1E" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round"/><path id="dm-p" d="" fill="#9C2C3F" fill-opacity=".85"/></svg></div></section>

<section class="dr-s"><div class="dr-w"><div class="dr-sh dr-sh-c"><span>Chapter three</span><h2>Myths of the drape</h2></div><div class="dr-myths">
<div><p class="dr-m">&ldquo;Draped clothes only suit tall people.&rdquo;</p><p class="dr-f">Proportion matters more than height. Shorter frames suit drapes that start higher, at the shoulder or waist, and stop above the ankle.</p></div>
<div><p class="dr-m">&ldquo;Heavier fabric always drapes better.&rdquo;</p><p class="dr-f">Weight helps, but fibre and weave matter more. A light silk can drape beautifully while a heavy canvas stands stiffly.</p></div>
<div><p class="dr-m">&ldquo;You need pins to keep a drape in place.&rdquo;</p><p class="dr-f">Most everyday drapes hold with a tuck, a knot or a belt. Pins are for formal sarees and fine, slippery cloth.</p></div>
<div><p class="dr-m">&ldquo;Drapes add bulk.&rdquo;</p><p class="dr-f">Soft vertical folds lengthen the body. Bulk comes from stiff fabric gathered horizontally.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="dr-s dr-dark"><div class="dr-w dr-meas"><svg viewBox="0 0 160 360" aria-hidden="true" class="dr-bod"><circle cx="80" cy="34" r="22" fill="none" stroke="#E9DED6" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M80 56v170M40 90h80M80 226l-30 120M80 226l30 120" stroke="#E9DED6" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/><path d="M48 86c30 40 60 80 70 150" stroke="#E0A6B1" stroke-width="6" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><div><div class="dr-sh"><span>Chapter four</span><h2>How much fabric?</h2><p>Your height and the drape you want decide the length.</p></div><div class="dr-form"><label for="mf-h">Your height (cm)<input id="mf-h" type="number" min="120" max="210" value="165"></label><label for="mf-s">Drape style<select id="mf-s"><option value="scarf">Long scarf</option><option value="shawl">Wrap shawl</option><option value="sarong">Sarong or wrap skirt</option><option value="saree">Saree (draped)</option></select></label></div><div class="dr-res dr-res-d" id="mf-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div></section>

<section class="dr-s"><div class="dr-w"><div class="dr-sh"><span>Chapter five</span><h2>A small glossary of drape</h2></div><dl class="dr-gl"><div><dt>Bias</dt><dd>Cut diagonally across the weave so fabric stretches and flows.</dd></div><div><dt>Cowl</dt><dd>A neckline that falls in soft, rounded folds.</dd></div><div><dt>Hand</dt><dd>How a fabric feels and moves in the hand.</dd></div><div><dt>Pallu</dt><dd>The decorated end of a saree, worn over the shoulder.</dd></div><div><dt>Pleat</dt><dd>A fold pressed or stitched in place.</dd></div><div><dt>Ruche</dt><dd>Fabric gathered in rows to create soft ripples.</dd></div><div><dt>Selvedge</dt><dd>The finished edge of woven fabric that does not fray.</dd></div><div><dt>Tuck</dt><dd>A fold stitched down to shape or hold fabric.</dd></div></dl></div></section>

<section class="dr-quote"><div class="dr-w"><blockquote>&ldquo;A good drape is never fixed. It answers every step you take.&rdquo;</blockquote></div></section>

